# Evidence hashes must resolve to identical bytes on Windows and Linux.
#
# manifest.json asserts a sha256 for every file in a sealed receipt directory,
# and those hashes are computed over the working tree. A file checked out CRLF
# on Windows and LF in CI cannot produce the same digest in both places, so a
# seal that verifies locally fails in CI and vice versa. PUBLISH.md step 5 and
# docs/DEPLOY.md steps 2 and 6 both depend on this holding.
#
# The previous version of this file listed nine specific paths. That allowlist
# silently excluded 45 hash-bearing files — every Cambridge, Kitchener,
# Waterloo and Woolwich ledger and citation audit, north-dumfries-on's
# sources.lock.json, all three receipts/north-dumfries-on/2026/*/manifest.json,
# and the whole web/src/data mirror. Enumerating paths does not scale with the
# fleet and fails open when a municipality is added, so normalization is now
# repo-wide and binaries are declared explicitly instead.

# Normalize every text file to LF, in the repository and in the working tree.
# eol=lf (not just text=auto) is required: the working tree is what gets hashed.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary — never normalized, never diffed as text.
*.pdf   binary
*.zip   binary
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.webp  binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
*.xlsx  binary
*.xlsm  binary
*.docx  binary
*.pptx  binary

# Windows shells require CRLF to parse these correctly. None are checked in
# today; the rule exists so adding one later cannot break it silently.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# PowerShell handles LF correctly and scripts/deploy_preview.ps1 is read by the
# release path, so it stays LF with everything else.
